## Editing discipline

- Prefer small, task-scoped diffs; match existing naming and DI patterns.
- Do not add unsolicited markdown files unless the user asks.
- Keep `docs/development-architecture.md` and this rule aligned when changing Docker, SDK, or architecture conventions.
- After each implementation iteration, run a cleanup pass on touched code before finishing.
- Cleanup must prioritize readability, naming clarity, small responsibilities, and low coupling (clean code style aligned with Robert C. Martin principles).
- Follow TDD by default for feature work: define/update tests first, implement behavior, then refactor while keeping tests green.
- Every new feature must include tests in the same iteration: unit tests are mandatory.
- E2E tests are required only when business scenarios or acceptance flows depend on end-to-end behavior.
- Integration tests are required when a feature crosses module or service boundaries.
- When touching legacy areas with missing coverage, add or extend tests for that area before finishing the task.
- Validation loop is mandatory for delivered changes: `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, and a smoke `dotnet run` check for app-facing behavior.
- Keep test taxonomy explicit and separated by structure:
  - unit tests under `src/UnitTests/<Project>/Unit/` and in `UnitTests.*` projects only;
  - integration tests under dedicated `src/IntegrationTests/<Project>/` and `IntegrationTests.*` projects only (when used);
  - e2e tests under dedicated `src/E2ETests/<Project>/` and `E2ETests.*` projects only.
- Do not colocate integration/e2e tests in unit-test projects, folders, or namespaces.
- Keep test flow explicit in CI/local runs:
  - run unit tests first (fast feedback gate),
  - run integration tests second when present (cross-module behavior gate),
  - run e2e last (full-system gate).
- Keep folder structure clean and responsibility-driven:
  - do not mix app-specific UI code into core libs unless intentionally shared;
  - organize code by cohesive modules (state/actions/reducer/view/theme for UI modules);
  - avoid dumping unrelated files into the same folder.
- Keep source files responsibility-scoped:
  - split files into cohesive units by intent (abstractions, models, services, systems, composition);
  - avoid "god files" with multiple unrelated top-level types;
  - prefer one primary top-level type per file unless types are tightly coupled and intentionally local.
- Keep `src/Apps/GameDesktop/Game.cs` as a top-level orchestration entry point:
  - only keep flow-level lifecycle coordination and high-level feature wiring there;
  - allow additions in `Game.cs` only when introducing/changing whole architecture capabilities (for example introducing UI as a platform-level feature);
  - implement feature details (input mapping, UI effects, state transitions, helpers, side effects) in dedicated lower-level modules/services under explicit architectural folders and namespaces.
- Keep folder and namespace structure aligned:
  - namespaces must mirror physical folder paths from the project root;
  - create semantic subfolders for grouped responsibilities only inside an existing bounded module (for example `Abstractions`, `Model`, `Sources`, `Composition`);
  - avoid flat folders when a module has multiple responsibility groups.
- Do not create new top-level `src/Libs/*` libraries for routine refactors.
  - First place code in the most appropriate existing library (`UI`, `Services`, `Systems`, `Features`, etc.) based on ownership;
  - create a new top-level library only when the user explicitly requests it or when architecture migration scope is explicitly approved.
- Prefer event-driven design when it improves decoupling or avoids repeated work:
  - emit explicit ECS events for state transitions that other systems consume (movement, UI intent, domain signals);
  - keep frame-driven loops only for inherently continuous behavior (rendering, animation ticks, physics stepping);
  - avoid event indirection when it adds complexity without measurable value.
- For each new feature, choose established and efficient algorithms/patterns:
  - default to well-known approaches with predictable complexity and maintainability;
  - document intentional tradeoffs when choosing simpler but slower paths;
  - evaluate reactive streams (for example Rx) only when asynchronous or multi-source event composition is required; do not add Rx to hot game-loop paths by default.
- Before adding new implementation, search for existing solutions in the repo, extract shared logic into common modules, and reuse it; avoid duplicate implementations.
- Before changing code, read and understand the current implementation path end-to-end (call chain + ownership) and then place changes at the lowest appropriate architectural level.
- Prefer established, well-documented libraries over custom implementations when they solve the problem sufficiently.
- For new external dependencies or major library choices, verify current best practices and version/reliability information from up-to-date official docs or release sources before implementation.
- Bias decisions toward feature value delivery and reduced maintenance burden: avoid writing/maintaining custom infrastructure when a mature library can safely cover the need.
- Naming must be explicit and human-readable across files, folders, namespaces, and test classes.
- Conventional abbreviations are allowed when they are standard and unambiguous (e.g. `UI`, `DTO`, `E2E`, `API`, `ID`).

## UI Architecture (FSD-inspired)

- Build UI with FSD-style boundaries and explicit responsibilities per layer.
- Preferred namespace/layer mapping:
  - `GameUi.App.*` — app-level orchestration and state transitions,
  - `GameUi.Pages.*` — page-level composition,
  - `GameUi.Widgets.*` — reusable visual blocks,
  - `GameUi.Features.*` — feature-specific interaction logic,
  - `GameUi.Entities.*` — UI-facing entities/value models,
  - `GameUi.Shared.*` — themes, styles, utilities, and shared primitives.
- Keep dependency direction top-down (`App -> Pages -> Widgets/Features -> Entities -> Shared`).
- Avoid cross-layer shortcuts that break modularity (for example, `Shared` must not depend on `Features`/`Pages`).
